WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:11.144 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:11.144 --> 00:00:13.078 line:0%
-May! Alright, alright, come on.

00:00:13.078 --> 00:00:15.081 line:0%
We can't stay here.
We gotta get out of this place.

00:00:15.081 --> 00:00:17.617 line:0%
We gotta move fast.
You gotta hold my hand

00:00:17.617 --> 00:00:19.352 line:0%
and move exactly where I move.
Okay?

00:00:19.352 --> 00:00:21.253 line:0%
-Okay.
-Now come on, come on.

00:00:21.253 --> 00:00:25.224
Gotta avoid the lights.
Now this way, this way. Come on.

00:00:25.224 --> 00:00:26.425
Come on. Keep moving!

00:00:26.425 --> 00:00:28.528
-We're back with Kurt
and Wyatt Russell.

00:00:28.528 --> 00:00:31.664
Another clip from "Monarch."

00:00:31.664 --> 00:00:34.333
So...

00:00:34.333 --> 00:00:35.869
People who haven't watched
the show might not know

00:00:35.869 --> 00:00:37.503
from the clip,
you play the same character

00:00:37.503 --> 00:00:39.205
at different parts
of your life in the show,

00:00:39.205 --> 00:00:41.974
so you actually didn't get
to shoot any scenes together?

00:00:41.974 --> 00:00:43.175
-No.
-We shot one,

00:00:43.175 --> 00:00:46.045
but it was not used.
-That's right.

00:00:46.045 --> 00:00:47.279
-No chemistry?

00:00:47.279 --> 00:00:49.182
"They just don't
seem like they like each other."

00:00:49.182 --> 00:00:51.650
[ Laughter ]

00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:54.053
And did you -- Was it pitched
to you guys as,

00:00:54.053 --> 00:00:56.155
"hey, you know,
obviously this makes sense.

00:00:56.155 --> 00:00:58.224
You look enough
alike that it would make sense

00:00:58.224 --> 00:00:59.525
that you'd be two people
in different times"?

00:00:59.525 --> 00:01:02.595
-Yeah. We have the same agent,

00:01:02.595 --> 00:01:05.198
and he called and he was like,
"Hey, you know, this is an idea.

00:01:05.198 --> 00:01:07.399
Ran across the casting director,
said, you know,

00:01:07.399 --> 00:01:08.701
had this great idea."

00:01:08.701 --> 00:01:11.971
And so we sort of thought,
"I don't know, maybe.

00:01:11.971 --> 00:01:13.940
Yeah, maybe it could be.
It could be the worst idea

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:15.475
of all time, too."

00:01:15.475 --> 00:01:17.877
As we went down
the rabbit hole, you know,

00:01:17.877 --> 00:01:19.879
we started thinking
might be a good idea.

00:01:19.879 --> 00:01:21.247
-Yeah. We did.

00:01:21.247 --> 00:01:23.650
We'd had the opportunity
to play a father and son before.

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:25.751
Nothing really struck our fancy.

00:01:25.751 --> 00:01:28.955
And, you know, just because
we're all -- in our family,

00:01:28.955 --> 00:01:30.223
a lot of us are
in the same business,

00:01:30.223 --> 00:01:33.425
and we just kind of go down
our own paths.

00:01:33.425 --> 00:01:35.594
But yet, I think everybody
in the family,

00:01:35.594 --> 00:01:38.832
we'd all like to work with each
other at some time or another.

00:01:38.832 --> 00:01:40.800
And this was a different kind
of an opportunity to be able

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.535
to play the same person.
It was also very challenging

00:01:42.535 --> 00:01:44.203
because you're not playing
father and son,

00:01:44.203 --> 00:01:45.538
you're playing
the same person.

00:01:45.538 --> 00:01:48.240
And I found myself
going down to Wyatt's set,

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:49.776
watching him.

00:01:49.776 --> 00:01:52.178
And I realized, yeah,
this is interesting.

00:01:52.178 --> 00:01:53.212
I stopped watching Wyatt

00:01:53.212 --> 00:01:54.680
and I started
just watching the actor,

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:56.049
and I said,
"This guy is good, man."

00:01:56.049 --> 00:01:58.618
[ Laughter ]

00:01:58.618 --> 00:02:00.219
-Thanks, Dad.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:02:00.219 --> 00:02:01.720
[ Laughter ]

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:04.790
-"Bet your dad
called you 'Tiger.'"

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:06.292
But I said, you know.
"Yeah, I got to

00:02:06.292 --> 00:02:08.227
see what he's doing here

00:02:08.227 --> 00:02:10.963
and kind of continue it on many,
many, many years later."

00:02:10.963 --> 00:02:14.834
-It was cool because,
like, we've been asked a lot,

00:02:14.834 --> 00:02:16.702
"What did you learn about your
dad" or "what did your dad --"

00:02:16.702 --> 00:02:18.771
It's like, well, I didn't really
learn anything about my dad

00:02:18.771 --> 00:02:21.273
because all I ever knew
was my dad,

00:02:21.273 --> 00:02:22.508
and he's the truth teller.

00:02:22.508 --> 00:02:24.343
He tells me the truth.
It's really boring.

00:02:24.343 --> 00:02:26.545
But, like, it's the way life is.
And it's amazing for me

00:02:26.545 --> 00:02:28.380
because I never had
to second-guess things.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:31.451
And all I ever remember
is he was doing "Tombstone"

00:02:31.451 --> 00:02:34.454
and there was a room,
a hotel room.

00:02:34.454 --> 00:02:37.222
We were -- I was there for
probably three weeks maybe.

00:02:37.222 --> 00:02:38.491
-Were you nine years old?
-Seven.

00:02:38.491 --> 00:02:40.260
-Seven.
-And there was a couch,

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:41.393
and he put me to sleep
on the couch.

00:02:41.393 --> 00:02:43.028
It was like 3 a.m.,

00:02:43.028 --> 00:02:45.364
and they'd gotten done
with a long day of shooting.

00:02:45.364 --> 00:02:49.368
He goes, smoking cigarets
at the table, starts writing,

00:02:49.368 --> 00:02:50.837
and he was like writing
for a couple hours.

00:02:50.837 --> 00:02:52.504
Then he'd go to sleep, wake up,
and I was like,

00:02:52.504 --> 00:02:54.040
"Oh, I guess that's what you do
when you work.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:55.608
You smoke cigarettes and write."

00:02:55.608 --> 00:02:57.210
[ Laughter ]

00:02:57.210 --> 00:03:00.412
And so all I ever knew
was the passion and energy

00:03:00.412 --> 00:03:01.681
that he brought to it.

00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:04.984
And seeing other people
get to see this now,

00:03:04.984 --> 00:03:08.488
at his age,
which is a very youthful 47...

00:03:08.488 --> 00:03:10.556
[ Laughter ]

00:03:10.556 --> 00:03:12.425
...that, like, it was just
really cool for me

00:03:12.425 --> 00:03:16.596
to see like people go, "Oh, wow,
holy S-H-I-T.

00:03:16.596 --> 00:03:17.797
Kurt Russell really
is the real deal."

00:03:17.797 --> 00:03:18.998
-Yeah.
-That was cool for me.

00:03:18.998 --> 00:03:20.099
-That's really awesome.

00:03:20.099 --> 00:03:22.235
And one of the nice things
about this,

00:03:22.235 --> 00:03:23.736
sometimes press
is hard for actors,

00:03:23.736 --> 00:03:25.738
but because you didn't shoot
together, this is the nice part.

00:03:25.738 --> 00:03:27.039
You actually get to go out
and hang out.

00:03:27.039 --> 00:03:28.975
-This is the most time we've got
to spend with each other

00:03:28.975 --> 00:03:31.877
since he was playing hockey
and in Vancouver,

00:03:31.877 --> 00:03:34.147
even before he went -- long
before he went into college.

00:03:34.147 --> 00:03:36.482
-So you you were both
Kurt Russell,

00:03:36.482 --> 00:03:38.851
the actor, and a hockey dad
at the same time,

00:03:38.851 --> 00:03:41.120
which is a real job.
-I was a good hockey dad.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:43.355
I was a very bad baseball dad.
-Okay, interesting.

00:03:43.355 --> 00:03:45.591
-But I was a good hockey dad
because I didn't know that game.

00:03:45.591 --> 00:03:47.092
I loved the game.

00:03:47.092 --> 00:03:49.128
And my only wish
and hope for him

00:03:49.128 --> 00:03:50.563
was that he wouldn't become
a goaltender.

00:03:50.563 --> 00:03:52.565
-Yeah.
-And I think it was the third

00:03:52.565 --> 00:03:54.367
time he stepped on the ice.
He was about 4 or 5.

00:03:54.367 --> 00:03:55.868
The coach looks up to me
and goes,

00:03:55.868 --> 00:03:57.069
"He wants to put the pads on."

00:03:57.069 --> 00:03:58.938
I said, "No, man."
[ Laughter ]

00:03:58.938 --> 00:04:00.706
"Just let him skate out
and have some fun."

00:04:00.706 --> 00:04:02.108
-Can't lose if you
don't get scored on.

00:04:02.108 --> 00:04:04.076
-That's what he said.
The next thing I know,

00:04:04.076 --> 00:04:05.311
the rest of the team's
down there.

00:04:05.311 --> 00:04:06.578
He's standing in front
of the goaltender,

00:04:06.578 --> 00:04:08.447
keeping that puck
out of the net.

00:04:08.447 --> 00:04:10.683
So he was born to get
in the net.

00:04:10.683 --> 00:04:12.251
-My dad never missed a game.

00:04:12.251 --> 00:04:14.553
-That's fantastic.
-It was awesome.

00:04:14.553 --> 00:04:15.722
-I would miss so many.

00:04:15.722 --> 00:04:18.057
[ Laughter ]

00:04:18.057 --> 00:04:20.593
I hope they get into sports
just so I can miss them.

00:04:20.593 --> 00:04:22.028
-Goldie made every one too.
I'll tell you what.

00:04:22.028 --> 00:04:23.863
-Oh, God love her.

00:04:23.863 --> 00:04:24.931
Another thing
about a show like this,

00:04:24.931 --> 00:04:27.467
you know, obviously,
it's sci-fi,

00:04:27.467 --> 00:04:30.703
a lot of special effects.
You do the work as actors.

00:04:30.703 --> 00:04:32.438
You must feel good
about what you do.

00:04:32.438 --> 00:04:33.672
And then you must have
felt so good

00:04:33.672 --> 00:04:35.208
when you actually saw
the finished product.

00:04:35.208 --> 00:04:37.043
And because, again,
you don't get to have

00:04:37.043 --> 00:04:38.911
the benefit of the final effects

00:04:38.911 --> 00:04:40.446
until you see it.
It must have been so exciting.

00:04:40.446 --> 00:04:44.150
-It was very exciting
and also very just affirming

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:46.652
because in my head
I was always going,

00:04:46.652 --> 00:04:49.054
"Well, if I suck, the monsters
are going to be sick."

00:04:49.054 --> 00:04:50.890
You know, the monsters
are going to be awesome

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:52.091
and that's the backstop.

00:04:52.091 --> 00:04:53.292
And then as you start
watching it, you're like,

00:04:53.292 --> 00:04:54.627
"I hope the monsters are good

00:04:54.627 --> 00:04:56.396
because I wasn't that good
in that scene."

00:04:56.396 --> 00:04:58.797
[ Laughter ]
Watching with a critical eye.

00:04:58.797 --> 00:05:01.300
But Apple was never going
to let it be anything but great.

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:03.702
And so I always had
a lot of fun.

00:05:03.702 --> 00:05:05.771
-Yeah. It is fun
to see the outcome of it,

00:05:05.771 --> 00:05:09.108
especially now -- What is it?
Friday the last one's on.

00:05:09.108 --> 00:05:12.744
And it's got a -- 8, 9, and 10
are bang-up finish on this thing

00:05:12.744 --> 00:05:14.313
and it's got a great last shot.
-Great ending.

00:05:14.313 --> 00:05:15.815
-It's got a great ending on it.

00:05:15.815 --> 00:05:17.283
-Really does have
a great last shot.

00:05:17.283 --> 00:05:18.618
-I can't wait to see it.
Thank you guys for being --

00:05:18.618 --> 00:05:20.018
What a joy to have you
both together.

00:05:20.018 --> 00:05:21.287
-Thank you.
-Hey, man, thanks for this.

00:05:21.287 --> 00:05:23.255
It's very sweet.
-Thank you, guys.

00:05:23.255 --> 00:05:25.657
That's Kurt Russell,
Wyatt Russell.

00:05:25.657 --> 00:05:27.226
Season finale of "Monarch"

00:05:27.226 --> 00:05:29.561
this Friday, January 12th on
Apple TV+.

00:05:29.561 --> 00:05:32.064
"Night Swim," fantastic.
-Oh, yeah.

00:05:32.064 --> 00:05:33.599
-In theaters now.
Stick around.

00:05:33.599 --> 00:05:34.901
We'll be right back
with David Chase.

